==================================================================
DAY 5 - PART B: LAB
Install kubectl and kind, build a 3-node cluster
Time: 90 minutes
==================================================================

PODMAN USERS (RHEL / Rocky / Alma): kind works best with Docker.
If you only have Podman, run kind as root with:
    export KIND_EXPERIMENTAL_PROVIDER=podman
and use "sudo" for kind commands. Installing Docker CE is simpler for
this course: https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/ (choose your distro)


STEP 1: Install kubectl
-----------------------
curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"
sudo install -o root -g root -m 0755 kubectl /usr/local/bin/kubectl
kubectl version --client

Expected: "Client Version: v1.xx.x"
(On ARM machines replace amd64 with arm64.)


STEP 2: Install kind
--------------------
curl -Lo ./kind https://kind.sigs.k8s.io/dl/latest/kind-linux-amd64
sudo install -m 0755 kind /usr/local/bin/kind
kind version


STEP 3: Shortcuts and autocomplete (saves hours later)
------------------------------------------------------
echo 'alias k=kubectl' >> ~/.bashrc
echo 'source <(kubectl completion bash)' >> ~/.bashrc
echo 'complete -o default -F __start_kubectl k' >> ~/.bashrc
source ~/.bashrc

If completion fails, install it: sudo dnf install -y bash-completion
(or: sudo apt install -y bash-completion), then open a new shell.

Test: type "k get no" and press TAB. It should complete to "nodes".


STEP 4: Create a 3-node cluster
-------------------------------
mkdir -p ~/k8s-journey/kind && cd ~/k8s-journey/kind

cat > kind-config.yaml <<EOF
kind: Cluster
apiVersion: kind.x-k8s.io/v1alpha4
nodes:
- role: control-plane
- role: worker
- role: worker
EOF

kind create cluster --name lab --config kind-config.yaml

This takes 1-3 minutes. Read the output: it creates the node
containers, writes the config, starts the control plane, installs the
CNI, installs a StorageClass, and joins the workers.


STEP 5: Explore the cluster
---------------------------
k cluster-info
k get nodes
k get nodes -o wide
k get namespaces
k get pods -A

You should see 3 nodes in "Ready" status. In namespace kube-system,
find these pods and match each one to Part A:

  kube-apiserver-lab-control-plane          -> API server
  etcd-lab-control-plane                    -> etcd
  kube-scheduler-lab-control-plane          -> scheduler
  kube-controller-manager-lab-control-plane -> controller manager
  kube-proxy-xxxxx   (one per node)         -> kube-proxy
  coredns-xxxxx                             -> cluster DNS (Week 3)
  kindnet-xxxxx      (one per node)         -> CNI plugin


STEP 6: Look at a node in detail
--------------------------------
k describe node lab-worker

Find these sections and note the values:
  - Capacity / Allocatable   (CPU, memory, max pods)
  - Conditions               (Ready, MemoryPressure, DiskPressure)
  - System Info              (Container Runtime Version: containerd://...)
  - Non-terminated Pods


STEP 7: Understand your kubeconfig
----------------------------------
k config view
k config current-context
k config get-contexts
ls -l ~/.kube/config

Identify the three sections: clusters, users, contexts.
A context = cluster + user (+ default namespace).
Current context should be "kind-lab".


STEP 8: See the cluster as containers
-------------------------------------
docker ps

You will see lab-control-plane, lab-worker, lab-worker2.
Each "node" is a container running systemd, kubelet and containerd.

docker exec -it lab-worker bash
  systemctl status kubelet --no-pager | head -5
  ps aux | grep -E "kubelet|containerd" | grep -v grep
  exit


STEP 9: Talk to the API server directly
---------------------------------------
k get --raw /healthz ; echo
k get --raw /version
k api-resources | head -20
k api-resources | wc -l

api-resources lists every object type the cluster knows.


STEP 10: See the control plane as static pods
---------------------------------------------
docker exec lab-control-plane ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests

These YAML files are read directly by the kubelet on the control-plane
node. That is how the control plane runs as pods (Week 6 goes deeper).


STEP 11: Save your work
-----------------------
cd ~/k8s-journey
git add . && git commit -m "day5: kind cluster"


TROUBLESHOOTING
---------------
- "permission denied ... docker.sock": run "newgrp docker" or log out
  and back in.
- kind create hangs or fails: check free RAM (free -h); you need about
  4 GB. For a lighter lab use a single node: kind create cluster --name lab
- "too many open files":
    sudo sysctl fs.inotify.max_user_watches=524288
    sudo sysctl fs.inotify.max_user_instances=512
